Subject: Runtime: zmark_shared, shared mappings that hold addresses only
Origin: Filnix
Upstream-Status: Not submitted

A MAP_SHARED or shmat mapping shares its bytes, but a pointer stored in it
keeps its capability in the storing process's private aux, so other processes
read it without one and trap. zmark_shared(p) marks a fresh shared mapping
(no aux yet) with FILC_OBJECT_FLAG_SHARED. A marked object never gets an aux.
Pointer loads from it, atomic or not, give a non-null address the object's
own capability and a null one none; pointer stores, CAS and exchange write the
address only and panic unless the pointer is null or has the object's
capability. memmove out of it gives each fully copied word the capability a
load would; memmove into it copies bytes only. ensure_aux panics for a marked object, so code
that does not know about the flag (other compilers, older outlines) fails
safe; compiled code calls filc_object_ensure_aux_ptr_or_shared_outline, which
returns NULL instead.

Design, invariants and review: filnix docs/zmark-shared.md. Regressions:
filnix tests/zmark-shared.nix (checks.staging-zmark-shared).

